CHAPTER I
GENERAL PROVISIONS
Article 1
In this Regional Regulation what is meant by:
1. Region is Cirebon Regency;
2. Regional Government is the Regent and Regional Apparatus of Cirebon Regency as elements of the administration of Cirebon Regency government;
3. Regional House of Representatives of Cirebon Regency, hereinafter referred to as DPRD, is a regional people's representative institution as an element of regional government administration;
4. Regent is the Regent of Cirebon Regency;
5. Regional Apparatus Work Unit hereinafter abbreviated as SKPD is the Apparatus of Cirebon Regency;
6. Discrimination is any distinction, harassment or exclusion either directly or indirectly based on human differences on the basis of religion, ethnicity, race, ethnic group, social status, economic status, gender, language, political convictions that result in reduction, deviation or elimination of recognition, implementation or use of human rights and basic freedoms in life both individual and collective in the political, economic, legal, social, cultural and other aspects of life;
7. Violence is any act unlawfully with or without using means against physical and psychological that causes danger to life, body or causes deprivation of a person's freedom;
8. Violence against women is any action based on gender differences that results in or may result in misery or suffering of women physically, sexually or psychologically, economic exploitation, social, including threats of certain actions, coercion or deprivation of freedom arbitrarily, whether occurring in the public sphere or in private life;
9. Violence against children is any act against a child that results in misery or suffering physically, mentally, sexually, psychologically, economic exploitation, social, including neglect and maltreatment that threatens bodily integrity and degrades the dignity of the child;
10. Domestic violence is any act against a person, women and children that results in misery or suffering physically, sexually, psychologically, economically and/or household neglect including threats to commit acts of coercion or deprivation of freedom unlawfully within the household scope;
11. Trafficking in Persons is the act of recruitment, transportation, harboring, sending, transfer, or receipt of a person by threat of violence, use of violence, abduction, confinement, forgery, fraud and abuse of power or position of vulnerability, debt bondage or giving payment or benefits, so as to obtain consent from the person holding control over that other person, whether conducted within the country or between countries, for the purpose of exploitation or resulting in a person being exploited;
12. Child is a person who has not reached the age of 18 (eighteen) years including a child still in the womb;
13. Child marriage is a marriage conducted through civil law, religion or custom with or without registration or official consent where one or both partners are children under the age of 18 years;
14. Woman is a person (human) who has a vagina, can menstruate, become pregnant, give birth to children, and breastfeed;
15. Victim of violence is an individual or group of people who experience suffering either physical, mental or emotional, economic loss, or experience neglect, reduction or deprivation of their basic rights, as a result of human rights (HAM) violations, or criminal acts as well as acts of violence committed either by state apparatus or by the state or regional government apparatus or by individuals, including victims are their heirs;
16. Protection is a series of fulfillment of the rights of women and child victims of violence and discrimination starting from prevention efforts, handling and empowerment;
17. Prevention is a direct effort carried out by the Regional Government to prevent acts of violence against women and children and indirect efforts;
18. Integrated Service Center for Women and Children Empowerment, hereinafter abbreviated as P2TP2A, is an Integrated Service Unit established by the Regional Government to provide services to women and child victims of violence comprehensively including information services, assistance and legal aid, counseling services, medical services and safe houses through referral;
19. Task Force for Prevention and Handling of Trafficking in Persons Crimes, hereinafter referred to as Task Force is a coordinative institution tasked with coordinating prevention and handling efforts of trafficking in persons crimes in Cirebon Regency;
20. Parents are biological father and/or mother, or step father and/or mother, or adoptive father and/or mother;
21. Family is a person who has a blood relationship in a straight line upward or downward and collateral line up to the third degree, or who has a marital relationship, or a person who is a dependent of a woman and/or child;
22. Community is individuals, families, groups, and social organizations and/or community organizations.
23. Child-Friendly Regency is a Regency that has a child rights-based development system through the integration of commitment and resources of government, community, business world and media that are planned comprehensively and sustainably in policies, programs and activities to ensure the fulfillment of children's rights and special protection of children.
24. Private Sector is Non-Governmental Institutions, Business World and Mass Media.

Article 3
The objectives of protection and empowerment of women and children are to:
a. Fulfill the rights of children;
b. Prevent acts of violence against women and children including human trafficking;
c. Eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
d. Protect and provide a sense of security for women and children;
e. Provide services to women and children victims of acts of violence;
f. Protect children from child marriage and conduct mediation on household disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household.

REGENT OF CIREBON,

Considering: a. that children are a trust and gift from God Almighty, in whom inherent dignity and worth as complete human beings reside, and who constitute the next generation of the nation's ideals and struggle, thus requiring protection and the widest possible opportunities for survival, growth and development in a proper manner;

b. that in connection with the still large number of women and children who need protection from various forms of violence, mistreatment, exploitation, and neglect, it is deemed necessary to have strategic efforts from the Regional Government and other parties who are obligated to provide protection to the aforementioned women and children;

c. that discrimination and violence against women and children constitute violations of human rights and crimes against humanity, therefore in the context of fulfilling the constitutional rights of women and children as referred to in letter b, the Regional Government may carry out integrated coordination functions regarding the implementation of its duties, authorities, and responsibilities;

d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection, Empowerment of Women and Children.

In View of: 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 14 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Areas Within the Province of West Java (State Gazette dated August 8, 1950) as amended by Law Number 4 of 1968 concerning the Establishment of Purwakarta Regency and Subang Regency by amending Law Number 14 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Areas Within the Province of West Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1968 Number 3, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 2851);

3. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235), as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 297);

4. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);

5. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);

6. Law Number 36 of 2009 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2009 Number 144, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5063);

7. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 224, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);

8. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);

9. Presidential Regulation Number 69 of 2008 concerning the Task Force for Prevention and Handling of the Crime of Trafficking in Persons;

10. Regulation of the Minister of Social Affairs Number 102/HUK/2007 concerning the Establishment and Operation of Shelters and Trauma Centers;

11. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 02 of 2008 concerning the Implementation of Women's Protection;

12. Regulation of the Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 03 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection;

13. Regulation of the Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards in the Field of Integrated Services for Women and Child Victims of Violence;

14. Regional Regulation of Cirebon Regency Number 9 of 2016 concerning the Determination of Concurrent Government Affairs that are the Authority of the Cirebon Regency Government (Regional Gazette of Cirebon Regency Year 2016 Number 9, Series D.6);

15. Regional Regulation of Cirebon Regency Number 10 of 2016 concerning Procedures for the Formulation of Regional Regulations (Regional Gazette of Cirebon Regency Year 2016 Number 10, Series E.3);

16. Regional Regulation of Cirebon Regency Number 12 of 2016 concerning the Establishment and Composition of Regional Apparatus of Cirebon Regency (Regional Gazette of Cirebon Regency Year 2016 Number 12, Series D.7).

With Mutual Agreement

CHAPTER VII
SERVICES FOR VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE
Article 13
(1) Forms of services provided to women and children victims of acts of violence are as follows:
a. Complaint services;
b. Health services;
c. Legal services;
d. Return;
e. Recovery, social reintegration, and medicolegal;
f. Identification services;
g. Psychological services; and
h. Shelter.
(2) Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the Government and implemented by SKPD whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. Social;
b. Economic;
c. Health;
d. Women's empowerment and child protection; and
e. Mental and spiritual.
(3) In the implementation of duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government cooperates with Government Agencies, other Regional Governments, and the community.
(4) Further provisions regarding procedures for services for the handling of women and children from acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2) and paragraph (3), shall be regulated by a Regent's Regulation.

Article 12
(1) Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 11, shall be implemented by SKPD whose duties and functions are in the field of:
a. Social affairs;
b. Health;
c. Education;
d. Manpower;
e. Women's empowerment and child protection;
f. Mental and spiritual matters; and
g. Public peace and order.
(2) Prevention of acts of violence by SKPD as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 8
(1) The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 7 letter a, include:
a. Establishing policies for the protection, empowerment of women and children;
b. Implementing policies, protection, empowerment of women and children from acts of violence established by the government;
c. Conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children;
d. Providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children;
e. Allocating budget for the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children, in accordance with regional financial capacity;
f. Fostering and supervising the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children; and
g. Realizing a child-friendly regency.
(2) In order to implement the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes programs and activities for the protection and empowerment action of women and children in the Regional Action Plan as a basis for SKPD in implementing the protection and empowerment of women and children.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), constitutes part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan (RPJMD).
(4) Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER VI
ROLE OF GOVERNMENT, PRIVATE SECTOR AND COMMUNITY INSTITUTIONS
Article 11
(1) To prevent acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government conducts empowerment and awareness raising to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling and realizing a Child-Friendly Regency.
(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness raising as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government undertakes efforts as follows:
a. Increasing the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. Providing accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. Providing employment opportunities for women;
d. Building community participation and concern towards prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. Building and providing an information system that is easily accessible;
f. Building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children;
g. Optimizing integrated service centers for women and children empowerment (P2TP2A) or by another designation as a protection post for women and children from acts of violence; and
h. Opening community-based complaint posts for the protection of women and children.

CHAPTER IV
RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE
Article 5
The rights of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the service process consist of/include:
a. The right to health recovery and the right to psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim;
b. The right to legal assistance;
c. The right to obtain information;
d. The right to social recovery;
e. The right to restitution (trafficking, rape);
f. The right to spiritual services;
g. The right to reintegration;
h. The right to shelter services;
i. The right to a companion.

CHAPTER II
PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES
Article 2
The protection and empowerment of women and children shall be conducted based on the principles of:
a. Humanity;
b. Justice and gender equality;
c. Safeguarding;
d. Best interests of victims;
e. Survival of the mother;
f. Survival and development of children;
g. Non-discrimination;
h. Confidentiality of victims;
i. Anti-violence;
j. Empowerment of victims;
k. Holistic.

Article 19
(1) In order to achieve the objectives of protection of women and children, cooperation may be conducted with:
a. Central Government;
b. Provincial Government/other Provinces;
c. Regency/City Government;
d. Non-governmental institutions; and
e. Community.
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. Exchange of data and information;
b. Recovery of victims of acts of violence;
c. Repatriation and social reintegration;
d. Provision of evidence and witnesses; and
e. Followed up in accordance with applicable law.
(3) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be set forth in the form of a Joint Agreement.

Article 6
Child victims of acts of violence, in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in Article 5, also obtain special rights, as follows;
a. The right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. The right to basic services;
c. The right to equal protection;
d. The right to be free from various stigma; and
e. The right to freedom.

Article 17
In addition to establishing the Integrated Service Center for the Empowerment of Women and Children (P2TP2A) or other designation as referred to in Article 16, in order to support the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence, the Regent shall establish:
a. Task force on human trafficking;
b. Task force on prevention of child marriage;
c. Regional action committee for the elimination of the worst forms of child labor;
d. Child-friendly district task force or other designation.

CHAPTER V
OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES
Article 7
Obligations and responsibilities in providing protection and Empowerment for women and children are the joint responsibility of:
a. Government/Regional Government and Government Institutions;
b. Private Sector and Community Institutions;
c. Society;
d. Family and or Parents.

CHAPTER III
CHILD-FRIENDLY REGENCY
Article 4
(1) In the context of implementing the Convention on the Rights of the Child and various laws and regulations and policies related to the fulfillment of children's rights and special protection of children, the Regional Government, private sector and community institutions make efforts to realize a Child-Friendly Regency.
(2) To realize a Child-Friendly Regency as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government, private sector and Community Institutions pay attention to children's rights related to the right to life, growth and development, protection and participation.
(3) The Regional Government, Private Sector and Community Institutions in realizing a Child-Friendly Regency are incorporated in the Child-Friendly Regency Task Force.
(4) The Child-Friendly Regency Task Force is responsible for the fulfillment of children's rights related to:
a. Civil rights and freedoms;
b. Family environment and alternative care;
c. Basic health and welfare;
d. Education, use of leisure time and arts and cultural activities;
e. Special protection of children.

