a. Social;
b. Economic;
c. Health;
d. Women's empowerment and child protection; and
e. Mental and spiritual.
(3) In the implementation of duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government cooperates with Government Agencies, other Regional Governments, and the community.
(4) Further provisions regarding procedures for services for the handling of women and children from acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2) and paragraph (3), shall be regulated by a Regent's Regulation.

Article 17
In addition to establishing the Integrated Service Center for the Empowerment of Women and Children (P2TP2A) or other designation as referred to in Article 16, in order to support the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence, the Regent shall establish:
a. Task force on human trafficking;
b. Task force on prevention of child marriage;
c. Regional action committee for the elimination of the worst forms of child labor;
d. Child-friendly district task force or other designation.

CHAPTER V
OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES
Article 7
Obligations and responsibilities in providing protection and Empowerment for women and children are the joint responsibility of:
a. Government/Regional Government and Government Institutions;
b. Private Sector and Community Institutions;
c. Society;
d. Family and or Parents.

CHAPTER I
GENERAL PROVISIONS
Article 1
In this Regional Regulation what is meant by:
1. Region is Cirebon Regency;
2. Regional Government is the Regent and Regional Apparatus of Cirebon Regency as elements of the administration of Cirebon Regency government;
3. Regional House of Representatives of Cirebon Regency, hereinafter referred to as DPRD, is a regional people's representative institution as an element of regional government administration;
4. Regent is the Regent of Cirebon Regency;
5. Regional Apparatus Work Unit hereinafter abbreviated as SKPD is the Apparatus of Cirebon Regency;
6. Discrimination is any distinction, harassment or exclusion either directly or indirectly based on human differences on the basis of religion, ethnicity, race, ethnic group, social status, economic status, gender, language, political convictions that result in reduction, deviation or elimination of recognition, implementation or use of human rights and basic freedoms in life both individual and collective in the political, economic, legal, social, cultural and other aspects of life;
7. Violence is any act unlawfully with or without using means against physical and psychological that causes danger to life, body or causes deprivation of a person's freedom;
8. Violence against women is any action based on gender differences that results in or may result in misery or suffering of women physically, sexually or psychologically, economic exploitation, social, including threats of certain actions, coercion or deprivation of freedom arbitrarily, whether occurring in the public sphere or in private life;
9. Violence against children is any act against a child that results in misery or suffering physically, mentally, sexually, psychologically, economic exploitation, social, including neglect and maltreatment that threatens bodily integrity and degrades the dignity of the child;
10. Domestic violence is any act against a person, women and children that results in misery or suffering physically, sexually, psychologically, economically and/or household neglect including threats to commit acts of coercion or deprivation of freedom unlawfully within the household scope;
11. Trafficking in Persons is the act of recruitment, transportation, harboring, sending, transfer, or receipt of a person by threat of violence, use of violence, abduction, confinement, forgery, fraud and abuse of power or position of vulnerability, debt bondage or giving payment or benefits, so as to obtain consent from the person holding control over that other person, whether conducted within the country or between countries, for the purpose of exploitation or resulting in a person being exploited;
12. Child is a person who has not reached the age of 18 (eighteen) years including a child still in the womb;
13. Child marriage is a marriage conducted through civil law, religion or custom with or without registration or official consent where one or both partners are children under the age of 18 years;
14. Woman is a person (human) who has a vagina, can menstruate, become pregnant, give birth to children, and breastfeed;
15. Victim of violence is an individual or group of people who experience suffering either physical, mental or emotional, economic loss, or experience neglect, reduction or deprivation of their basic rights, as a result of human rights (HAM) violations, or criminal acts as well as acts of violence committed either by state apparatus or by the state or regional government apparatus or by individuals, including victims are their heirs;
16. Protection is a series of fulfillment of the rights of women and child victims of violence and discrimination starting from prevention efforts, handling and empowerment;
17. Prevention is a direct effort carried out by the Regional Government to prevent acts of violence against women and children and indirect efforts;
18. Integrated Service Center for Women and Children Empowerment, hereinafter abbreviated as P2TP2A, is an Integrated Service Unit established by the Regional Government to provide services to women and child victims of violence comprehensively including information services, assistance and legal aid, counseling services, medical services and safe houses through referral;
19. Task Force for Prevention and Handling of Trafficking in Persons Crimes, hereinafter referred to as Task Force is a coordinative institution tasked with coordinating prevention and handling efforts of trafficking in persons crimes in Cirebon Regency;
20. Parents are biological father and/or mother, or step father and/or mother, or adoptive father and/or mother;
21. Family is a person who has a blood relationship in a straight line upward or downward and collateral line up to the third degree, or who has a marital relationship, or a person who is a dependent of a woman and/or child;
22. Community is individuals, families, groups, and social organizations and/or community organizations.
23. Child-Friendly Regency is a Regency that has a child rights-based development system through the integration of commitment and resources of government, community, business world and media that are planned comprehensively and sustainably in policies, programs and activities to ensure the fulfillment of children's rights and special protection of children.
24. Private Sector is Non-Governmental Institutions, Business World and Mass Media.

Article 12
