CHAPTER VI
ROLE OF GOVERNMENT, PRIVATE SECTOR AND COMMUNITY INSTITUTIONS
Article 11
(1) To prevent acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government conducts empowerment and awareness raising to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling and realizing a Child-Friendly Regency.
(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness raising as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government undertakes efforts as follows:
a. Increasing the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. Providing accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. Providing employment opportunities for women;
d. Building community participation and concern towards prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. Building and providing an information system that is easily accessible;
f. Building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children;
g. Optimizing integrated service centers for women and children empowerment (P2TP2A) or by another designation as a protection post for women and children from acts of violence; and
h. Opening community-based complaint posts for the protection of women and children.

CHAPTER III
CHILD-FRIENDLY REGENCY
Article 4
(1) In the context of implementing the Convention on the Rights of the Child and various laws and regulations and policies related to the fulfillment of children's rights and special protection of children, the Regional Government, private sector and community institutions make efforts to realize a Child-Friendly Regency.
(2) To realize a Child-Friendly Regency as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government, private sector and Community Institutions pay attention to children's rights related to the right to life, growth and development, protection and participation.
(3) The Regional Government, Private Sector and Community Institutions in realizing a Child-Friendly Regency are incorporated in the Child-Friendly Regency Task Force.
(4) The Child-Friendly Regency Task Force is responsible for the fulfillment of children's rights related to:
a. Civil rights and freedoms;
b. Family environment and alternative care;
c. Basic health and welfare;
d. Education, use of leisure time and arts and cultural activities;
e. Special protection of children.

Article 12
